 "expert_rate": 0.9216589861751152,
 "note": "Same task, observation, action, BC network and training recipe for both end effectors; only the hand differs (the dexterous hand uses a weld grasp assist, the YAM gripper a purely frictional grasp). Expert: success while collecting 200 demonstrations. BC: 3 training seeds x 100 held-out seeds (100000-100099); error bars are 95% intervals, the wider of the binomial interval and the spread across training seeds. Diffusion Policy and ACT rows: image-based policies (head + wrist RGB, joint angles), 1 training seed, 50 held-out episodes in the real-robot stand-in domain (shifted visuals and dynamics); error bars are the 95% Wilson interval. Touch in the Wild and DexScrew rows: FlexiTac tactile YAM gripper, 50 held-out seeds per condition; bump = the nut is pushed 3 cm along the pad face as the gripper first closes on it; shifted physics = heavier, slipperier nut, weaker arm gains and 2 control steps of latency. Ideal column: the same policy on the nominal simulator with nothing randomised or perturbed (default appearance and dynamics, no latency, no noise, no bump), 50 held-out seeds; for BC and the scripted experts, which were only ever evaluated that way, it repeats the Success column."
}